1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,150
This is the Angel of
Independence, in Mexico City.

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:07,990
When it opened in nineteen ten,
you climbed nine steps to reach it.

3
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:09,450
Today, there are twenty-three.

4
00:00:10,534 --> 00:00:11,984
Nobody built it taller.

5
00:00:12,254 --> 00:00:14,724
The column stands on
thousands of wooden piles,

6
00:00:14,854 --> 00:00:18,064
so it sinks too, but far more
slowly than the street around it.

7
00:00:18,804 --> 00:00:23,214
In the worst parts of this city, the ground
drops up to forty centimeters a year.

8
00:00:23,424 --> 00:00:24,474
That's more than a foot.

9
00:00:24,644 --> 00:00:26,694
And the height it loses never comes back.

10
00:00:27,476 --> 00:00:31,316
So how do you run a city of more than
twenty million people on ground that keeps

11
00:00:31,356 --> 00:00:32,206
falling away?

12
00:00:32,436 --> 00:00:34,066
And why can't anyone stop it?

13
00:00:36,201 --> 00:00:37,531
Here's the short version.

14
00:00:37,861 --> 00:00:40,471
Mexico City sits on the
bottom of an old lake.

15
00:00:40,641 --> 00:00:43,451
Under its streets lies a
thick layer of soft clay,

16
00:00:43,581 --> 00:00:45,851
and that clay is about
seventy percent water.

17
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:50,600
For more than a century, the city has
been drinking the water underneath it.

18
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:53,240
About seventy percent of
its supply is groundwater.

19
00:00:54,274 --> 00:00:56,704
Pump the water out, and
the clay squeezes flat.

20
00:00:56,854 --> 00:00:58,484
Think of a wet sponge under a brick.

21
00:00:58,574 --> 00:01:00,404
Everything on top goes down with it.

22
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:02,980
It has already gone down a long way.

23
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:06,970
In nineteen ten, the city center stood
almost two meters above the level of the old

24
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:07,440
lake.

25
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:10,880
By nineteen fifty-nine, it was
five and a half meters below it.

26
00:01:11,070 --> 00:01:12,920
The city had sunk below its own lake.

27
00:01:12,990 --> 00:01:16,060
And that's why, today, this city
has to pump its sewage uphill.

28
00:01:18,877 --> 00:01:22,647
The Aztec capital, Tenochtitlan,
stood on an island in that lake.

29
00:01:22,897 --> 00:01:25,027
The Spanish built their city on its ruins,

30
00:01:25,157 --> 00:01:26,567
and the lake kept flooding it.

31
00:01:26,797 --> 00:01:31,267
In sixteen twenty-nine, parts of the city
stayed under water for about five years.

32
00:01:32,197 --> 00:01:34,727
So, to survive, they
decided to kill the lake.

33
00:01:34,937 --> 00:01:37,607
It took almost three centuries
of canals and tunnels.

34
00:01:37,997 --> 00:01:40,037
In nineteen hundred,
the Gran Canal opened,

35
00:01:40,077 --> 00:01:41,947
and most of the lake drained away.

36
00:01:42,858 --> 00:01:45,908
By then, the city was already
drinking from hundreds of wells,

37
00:01:46,058 --> 00:01:47,948
sunk straight into the old lakebed.

38
00:01:48,526 --> 00:01:52,486
In nineteen twenty-five, an engineer named
Roberto Gayol compared new measurements

39
00:01:52,526 --> 00:01:54,816
with ones taken almost
fifty years earlier.

40
00:01:55,026 --> 00:01:56,196
The city center had dropped.

41
00:01:56,426 --> 00:01:57,856
He warned that the city was sinking.

42
00:01:58,186 --> 00:02:00,616
It took more than twenty
years for anyone to prove why.

43
00:02:00,846 --> 00:02:04,406
In nineteen forty-seven, engineer Nabor
Carrillo showed that the pumping was

44
00:02:04,446 --> 00:02:05,336
squeezing the clay.

45
00:02:07,667 --> 00:02:09,837
When the ground sinks
unevenly, buildings tilt.

46
00:02:10,245 --> 00:02:14,015
The Metropolitan Cathedral was already
sinking while it was being built.

47
00:02:14,205 --> 00:02:17,745
Its builders hid it, wedging in
stones and making the cornices uneven,

48
00:02:17,785 --> 00:02:19,175
so it would look straight.

49
00:02:20,085 --> 00:02:24,045
By nineteen eighty-nine, one end of the
cathedral was almost two and a half meters

50
00:02:24,085 --> 00:02:25,215
lower than the other.

51
00:02:25,902 --> 00:02:28,792
They couldn't lift the cathedral,
so they lowered the high side.

52
00:02:29,462 --> 00:02:32,842
Workers dug thirty-two shafts, and
through narrow pipes they pulled out clay,

53
00:02:32,882 --> 00:02:35,732
a little at a time, about
one and a half million times.

54
00:02:36,082 --> 00:02:39,132
In five years, the high side
came down by almost a meter.

55
00:02:40,095 --> 00:02:42,205
It's still sinking, along
with the whole city.

56
00:02:42,395 --> 00:02:44,045
But now it sinks almost evenly.

57
00:02:44,697 --> 00:02:49,057
A few blocks away, the Palace of Fine
Arts has sunk so far that its original

58
00:02:49,097 --> 00:02:50,907
entrance hall is now the basement.

59
00:02:51,657 --> 00:02:54,247
Visitors walk in through what
used to be the floor above.

60
00:02:57,138 --> 00:02:59,158
Remember the city sinking
below its own lake?

61
00:02:59,198 --> 00:03:00,228
That broke the drains.

62
00:03:00,822 --> 00:03:02,692
Sewage only flows downhill.

63
00:03:02,862 --> 00:03:07,082
But by the nineteen fifties, the city's
sewers sat four meters below the canal they

64
00:03:07,122 --> 00:03:08,632
were supposed to empty into.

65
00:03:08,922 --> 00:03:11,112
In the floods of nineteen
fifty and fifty-one,

66
00:03:11,262 --> 00:03:13,212
people crossed the city center in boats.

67
00:03:13,694 --> 00:03:16,744
So Mexico City started
pumping its sewage uphill.

68
00:03:17,054 --> 00:03:20,644
And in nineteen sixty-seven,
engineers made a radical decision:

69
00:03:20,934 --> 00:03:23,644
dig drains so deep that
however far the city sank,

70
00:03:23,734 --> 00:03:25,064
they would never stop working.

71
00:03:25,998 --> 00:03:28,788
The first deep tunnel opened
in nineteen seventy-five,

72
00:03:29,158 --> 00:03:31,748
with some shafts more than
two hundred meters deep.

73
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:35,070
But a single tunnel can't
be shut down for repairs.

74
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:37,590
So in twenty nineteen,
a second one opened,

75
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:39,310
sixty-two kilometers long.

76
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:41,650
It's the longest sewage
tunnel in the world.

77
00:03:44,543 --> 00:03:48,373
Back in nineteen forty, the city had
banned new wells in the old center.

78
00:03:48,663 --> 00:03:50,913
Later, it moved its
pumping out to the edges.

79
00:03:51,363 --> 00:03:55,463
Downtown, the sinking slowed, from up
to half a meter a year to ten or fifteen

80
00:03:55,503 --> 00:03:56,113
centimeters.

81
00:03:57,179 --> 00:03:59,409
But the water still had
to come from somewhere.

82
00:03:59,459 --> 00:04:01,509
So the edges started sinking faster.

83
00:04:01,839 --> 00:04:05,009
In Neza, a suburb on the old
lakebed east of the city,

84
00:04:05,279 --> 00:04:08,389
parts of the ground were dropping
about three centimeters a month.

85
00:04:09,299 --> 00:04:13,739
Today, about two point seven million people
live around the city in sunken bowls,

86
00:04:13,779 --> 00:04:16,349
where rainwater collects,
and floods are worse.

87
00:04:16,799 --> 00:04:20,439
Another one and a half million live where
the sinking tears open cracks in the

88
00:04:20,479 --> 00:04:20,909
ground.

89
00:04:23,858 --> 00:04:25,028
And here's the paradox.

90
00:04:25,378 --> 00:04:27,488
A city built on a lake
is running out of water.

91
00:04:28,076 --> 00:04:31,826
More than a quarter of its water is
pumped from reservoirs in the mountains,

92
00:04:31,916 --> 00:04:34,186
lifted more than eleven
hundred meters uphill.

93
00:04:34,596 --> 00:04:36,906
In twenty twenty-four,
after a long drought,

94
00:04:36,996 --> 00:04:39,346
those reservoirs fell
to about a quarter full,

95
00:04:39,416 --> 00:04:41,786
and some homes got water
for an hour a week.

96
00:04:42,626 --> 00:04:46,316
Sinking ground cracks the pipes,
and between leaks and illegal taps,

97
00:04:46,366 --> 00:04:49,076
up to forty percent of the
water never reaches a home.

98
00:04:51,836 --> 00:04:53,626
So, can Mexico City stop the sinking?

99
00:04:54,137 --> 00:04:54,967
Not completely.

100
00:04:55,117 --> 00:04:56,637
The lost height is gone for good.

101
00:04:56,677 --> 00:05:00,607
And the clay is still squeezing out
water from decades of past pumping.

102
00:05:00,937 --> 00:05:04,487
How fast it sinks now depends
mostly on how thick the clay is.

103
00:05:05,324 --> 00:05:08,264
But it can stop making things
worse, and it has done it before,

104
00:05:08,304 --> 00:05:09,494
in the old center.

105
00:05:09,984 --> 00:05:12,254
Now it's fixing leaks,
and since twenty nineteen,

106
00:05:12,544 --> 00:05:15,934
it has put more than seventy thousand
rain collectors on people's roofs.

107
00:05:18,888 --> 00:05:21,388
Mexico City drained the
lake it was built on,

108
00:05:21,428 --> 00:05:23,498
and then drank the water out of the mud.

109
00:05:23,548 --> 00:05:27,598
So the city fell below its own lake,
and now even its sewage has to climb.

110
00:05:28,519 --> 00:05:30,149
Which city should we explain next?

111
00:05:30,259 --> 00:05:31,049
Tell me in the comments.

112
00:05:31,662 --> 00:05:35,012
And the Angel of Independence still
stands at the heart of it all.

113
00:05:35,182 --> 00:05:38,322
Over the years, the city has
added step after step at its feet,

114
00:05:38,362 --> 00:05:40,492
so people can keep climbing up to it.

115
00:05:40,642 --> 00:05:41,772
Fourteen so far.

116
00:05:41,862 --> 00:05:42,532
And counting.

117
00:05:42,762 --> 00:05:43,892
This was How It's Run.
